From e39b7ddf8551c9a4a151f8f3c928f9cabbe38e25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:16:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] host_change_feed: resolve the state topic per
 subscriber profile; detach the probe thread

Follow-up to PR #2633 review t_01a1d6a08990466b nit 1 (card t_01a1d6b6cf22e01d).

Defect (latent: no loop subscribes to `state` yet). `feed()` is a process-wide singleton.
Its probe thread was started with `spawn_context_thread` on the first `subscribe`, so it
captured that caller's contextvars, and `default_probe` resolved state.db through
`_default_db_path -> get_hermes_home()` in that captured context. In a multi-profile gateway,
a loop of any other profile subscribing to `state` watched the first subscriber's state.db.
A first subscribe inside an ACTIVE_SCOPE also made the probe thread a TrackedThread owned
(and accounted) by that scope.

Fix:
- `subscribe()` resolves the bare `state` topic in the subscriber's own context to
  `state:<abs path>` (`state_topic()`), and reads that db's key at once as its baseline
  (no spurious wake on the next probe, no change after subscribe missed).
- `poll_once()` reads every state.db some live subscription names (and only those),
  independently of the host probe; `default_probe` no longer reads state.db.
  An unreadable state.db is UNKNOWN (fail-open: its subscribers wake).
- `start()` builds the probe thread in an empty `contextvars.Context()` (passive), so no
  profile home, secret scope or work scope crosses into it.

Tests (tests/hermes_cli/test_host_change_feed.py), RED on main (5 failed) -> GREEN (19 passed):
- two subscribers bound to different HERMES_HOME contexts each wake only on their own
  state.db data_version change;
- a state.db is probed only while subscribed; an unreadable one wakes its subscriber;
- a first subscribe inside an active work scope does not bind the probe thread to it
  (not a TrackedThread, scope accounting back to 0, thread sees no scope and no home).
